  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "The equation $3{x}^{2}-4x+1=0$ can be written in the form $3(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-4x+1 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4x & =-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4x & =-1 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}x) & =-1 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}x+{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}] & =-1 \\\\ 3[{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{4}{9}] & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}-3\\cdot \\frac{4}{9} & =-1 \\\\ 3{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3} & =-1 \\\\ 3{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} & =-1+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 3{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $q=\\frac{1}{3}.$",
